"K. Bailey" schrieb:
> 
> This is random thinking about game balance. I
> used to think reflection was too strong, now I think
> it's too easy to get.

It's a lot easier to get than previously, but to be _too_ easy, it
would have to somehow break game balance. As far as i can tell, maybe
one out of three deaths between the oracle and the castle could be
prevented by reflection alone. The amulet from Sokoban cuts out
slightly under half of these deaths. This is pretty powerful, roughly
equivalent to one early wish (and early wishes aren't incredibly rare
- there's more sources than just wands), and quite a big reward for
pushing some boulders. I still think it's just barely acceptable.

> I think if you can get a character to the oracle
> alive (not all that difficult of a task) you can
> complete sokoban barring YA*Ds and massive boulder
> follies. So reflection is much more common between
> oracle and castle than ever before. This makes many
> monsters almost harmless,

Not very many. It mainly reduces the threat posed by monsters carrying
wands (not many dragons in this area).

 and those that are still
> dangerous would probably be more dangerous if they
> never wasted their breath so to speak.

Monsters with breath attack never lose a turn when breathing, and they
never breathe when in melee range (barring displacement?).

> The "oR is a key ascension kit item.

As is the bag of holding.

> It's just too good a reward for the effort. The
> bag of holding I don't mind since I hate inventory
> management.

The amulet of reflection is only _too_ good if it's significantly
better than the bag. I wouldn't argue with that (for me, by the time i
reach Sokoban, reflection is roughly 100 times as desirable as a bag
of holding), but i think in general the two are considered roughly
equivalent - some folks even consider the bag more desirable <boggle>.

> So solutions might include:
> 
> 1. Make sokoban harder. Reduce the ways of recovering
> from mistakes (no squeezing past boulders, boulders
> cannot be pickaxed, 0-1 ?oE at 3rd level not 1st). Or
> penalize more for mistakes (bigger luck hits).

Experienced players get through without errors anyway. This would only
penalise newbies and players who just won't get their minds around the
Sokoban concept well enough. The penalties are big enough already, and
making recovery too hard could actually kill characters who
accidentally block their ways out of a level.

> Or
> reduce or remove food from sokoban making hunger a
> factor.

The food is usually part of the "treasure" Sokoban provides, this
would only make the branch on the whole less rewarding, it won't cause
players to abstain from Sokoban because they're afraid of hunger.

> Or add nastier monsters (nasty breath weapon
> monsters on the amulet level would be poetic).

Are Sokoban levels taken into account the normal way when calculating
monster difficulty - i.e. the further you advance, the
shallower/easier the environment? Considering all Sokoban levels as
equivalent to the starting level (or even as _deeper_ the further you
go) could make the branch more challenging, maybe even _too_
challenging.

> 2. Replace the amulet or reduce its probability
> by having more reward level possibilities. Magic
> marker? Wand of polymorph? Nice potions?

All of which must be used up to be useful - the bag or amulet should
last forever (magic marker and wand would also break an official
conduct or two when used, but that's beside the point). Also, the
current Sokoban rewards are items that can never be part of a
character's starting equipment, can be used by everybody and are
roughly equally useful for everyone. 
Doesn't leave many choices for alternative Sokoban rewards - magic
lamps (roughly as good as the amulet), magic whistles (roughly as good
as the bag); some of the rings would be interesting choices, but they
can be part of a starting inventory (Wizard, Monk [?]), and rings are
part of the standard Sokoban treasure anyway.

> Personally I don't mind the (BoH as a reward item
> since I despise inventory management as an obstacle.

The bag is only justified if it isn't overpowering. Inventory
management _is_ an important part of the game; if it were an arbitrary
inconvenience, it could just be removed by giving infinite inventory
and near-infinite carrying capicity (i.e. the ADOM way of inventory
"management", only limited by the availability of items in general and
the capacity of the player to find the desired item in 20+ pages of
misc. junk).

